<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Union for National Progress</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as UPRONA, Union pour le progrès national</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Union for National Progress (UPRONA) is a Burundian political party founded in the late 1950s and led to victory in the 1961 elections by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-rwagasore">Prince Louis Rwagasore</a>. After independence it became the single ruling party, dominated by the Tutsi military under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michel-micombero">Michel Micombero</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-baptiste-bagaza">Jean-Baptiste Bagaza</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-buyoya">Pierre Buyoya</a>. It lost power in the multiparty election of 1993 and later took part in power-sharing governments.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/union-for-national-progress.jpg" alt="Union for National Progress" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MrPenguin20 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_UPRONA.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Sources differ on the party's founding. The political scientist Warren Weinstein dates it to shortly after a meeting of customary chiefs and clergy convened in 1958 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-rwagasore">Prince Louis Rwagasore</a> and Léopold Biha; Biha said it was created in 1957 to protest a Belgian reorganisation that disempowered the monarchy; Governor Jean-Paul Harroy said Rwagasore founded it in late 1959. The colonial administration recognised it as a political party on 7 January 1960.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Rwagasore controlled the movement, though his family tie to the Mwami barred him from party office and he officially served only as an adviser. He sought a mass party across regions, ethnic groups and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">castes</a> and, wary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutu</a>–Tutsi conflict in Ruanda, divided party posts evenly between Hutu and Tutsi, though Tutsi held the most important offices. The party depended on his charisma and had weak organisation; many of the chiefs who first supported it, Biha among them, left, and seminary graduates, évolués and younger chiefs took their place.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before the municipal elections of 1960 the Belgian administration, fearing communist sympathies in the party, placed Rwagasore under house arrest, and the party boycotted the vote. In the legislative elections of 18 September 1961 it won 58 of 64 seats and Rwagasore became prime minister, but he was assassinated in October in a plot conceived by figures of the Christian Democratic Party. The party then split into a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casablanca">Casablanca</a> faction dominated by Tutsis and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monrovia">Monrovia</a> faction led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutus</a>, and three of its Hutu members, among them Pierre Ngendandumwe, became prime minister.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michel-micombero">Michel Micombero</a> took over the party after 1966, and it became a pillar of the military governments that ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burundi">Burundi</a> until 1993, when it came second in contested elections to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melchior-ndadaye">Melchior Ndadaye</a>'s FRODEBU. Its president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-buyoya">Pierre Buyoya</a> handed power to Domitien Ndayizeye of the Front for Democracy in Burundi on 30 April 2003, and in the legislative elections of 2005 the party won 15 of 118 seats. In 2010 it boycotted the presidential and councillors' elections but took part in the legislative ones.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Union for National Progress” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Prince Louis Rwagasore, eldest son of Mwambutsa IV, who led the nationalist party <b>UPRONA</b> to victory in 1961, is honoured as the hero of independence, and the anniversary of his death, 13 October, is a public holiday.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-burundi">Kingdom of Burundi · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Power rested with a Tutsi-dominated army and the single party <b>UPRONA</b>, founded by the independence hero Prince Louis Rwagasore, who had been assassinated in 1961.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-burundi">Republic of Burundi</a></li></ul></section>
